Code evidence of bypass:
- `PutObjectAcl::AuthenticatedRead` now adds an `AuthenticatedUsers` READ grant (crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:5429-5451).
- `AclGrants::allows_authenticated_users` accepts those grants for permission checks (crates/s3-types/src/lib.rs:483-488).
- `requester_has_acl_permission` uses `allows_authenticated_users` for any requester with a canonical user id (i.e., any authenticated caller) (crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:3936-3944).
- `requester_can_read_object` grants access if `requester_has_acl_permission` succeeds, and only gates the separate `object.public_read` path with `IgnorePublicAcls` (crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:3977-3987). The `public_read` flag itself is derived solely from `allows_all_users` (AllUsers grants) (crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:3947-3948). `IgnorePublicAcls` is only applied to those public_read/public_write paths (crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:4127-4146).

This means an object with the `authenticated-read` ACL (AuthenticatedUsers READ grant) is still readable by any authenticated account even when `IgnorePublicAcls=true`, because that setting never gates `allows_authenticated_users`. This matches the reported access-control bypass.

