# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: low | Policy adjusted: low
## Rationale
Authorization bypass is real in runtime code and affects object confidentiality across accounts, but it requires specific ACL configuration and authenticated access. Impact is significant but not catastrophic in the typical single-node deployment model; thus medium severity is appropriate.
## Likelihood
medium - Requires AuthenticatedRead ACLs and IgnorePublicAcls to be enabled; exploitation is straightforward for any authenticated account once these conditions hold.
## Impact
medium - Confidentiality impact: objects intended to be inaccessible when IgnorePublicAcls is enabled can be read by any authenticated account if AuthenticatedRead ACLs are set.
## Assumptions
- No deployment manifests were found; exposure depends on runtime configuration (ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR can bind beyond localhost).
- Bucket/object ACLs are used in deployment, and IgnorePublicAcls is relied upon to disable public ACLs.
- Multiple authenticated accounts can exist in the same deployment (cross-account read is meaningful).
- Object has AuthenticatedRead ACL (AuthenticatedUsers READ grant).
- Bucket public access block has IgnorePublicAcls=true.
- Attacker has a valid authenticated account (canonical user id).
## Path
n1 -> n2 -> n3 -> n4
## Path evidence
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:3930-3988` - requester_has_acl_permission accepts AuthenticatedUsers grants and requester_can_read_object does not gate that path with IgnorePublicAcls.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:4119-4152` - IgnorePublicAcls is only checked for public_read/public_write paths.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:5418-5453` - AuthenticatedRead canned ACL adds AuthenticatedUsers READ grant.
- `crates/s3-types/src/lib.rs:472-494` - allows_authenticated_users treats AuthenticatedUsers grants as valid for permission checks.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:55-70` - Default listen address is 127.0.0.1:9000 but can be overridden via ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR (exposure depends on runtime config).
## Narrative
The coordinator authorizes object reads via requester_has_acl_permission, which now accepts AuthenticatedUsers grants. requester_can_read_object only applies IgnorePublicAcls to the public_read (AllUsers) path, so AuthenticatedUsers grants remain effective even when IgnorePublicAcls=true. This allows any authenticated account to read objects with AuthenticatedRead ACLs despite public ACLs being configured to be ignored.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication
- ACL enforcement
- Public access block (IgnorePublicAcls)
## Blindspots
- No deployment/IaC manifests to confirm internet exposure or multi-tenant use.
- No runtime PoC executed due to build/tooling limitations; conclusions are based on static code paths.

# Follow-up note
Subsequent AWS validation showed the main `IgnorePublicAcls` conclusion in this finding was incorrect for S3 parity. On AWS, an alternate authenticated account can still read an object protected by the `authenticated-read` canned ACL after `IgnorePublicAcls=true`; that setting suppresses the anonymous `AllUsers` path but does not disable `AuthenticatedUsers` ACL access. As a result, the reported `IgnorePublicAcls` bypass is not an AWS-behavior bug in Argmin.

A separate `BlockPublicAcls` question was validated independently against AWS and does behave differently: write-time validation should treat `AuthenticatedRead` / `AuthenticatedUsers` as public ACLs. Focused AWS-backed tests confirmed that `BlockPublicAcls=true` rejects:
- canned `authenticated-read`
- explicit `AuthenticatedUsers` bucket ACL XML grants
- explicit `AuthenticatedUsers` object ACL XML grants
- `x-amz-grant-read` headers targeting `AuthenticatedUsers`

So the correct split is:
- `IgnorePublicAcls` portion of this finding: invalid for AWS parity
- `BlockPublicAcls` treatment of `AuthenticatedRead` / `AuthenticatedUsers`: valid AWS behavior, but it is a separate issue from the original claim above
